{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfdj1oEG1SJLEVNbHazUc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written `verdict.json` with an overall verdict of **upheld**: I couldn't find a reason any of the three checks is false. I only read files and wrote that one file. I didn't run any Python scripts.\n\n- **C02.1 holds.** The manifest, prompts and config are in the repo and loaded from local paths. The Dockerfile copies them into the image. CODEOWNERS covers `/prompts/`, `/config/`, `/release/`, `/infra/`, `/k8s/` and `/.github/`. Branch protection requires code owner review and 2 approvals.\n- **C02.2 holds.** The deploy role can be assumed only from the `production` and `production-emergency` environments, and both need `agent-platform-leads` reviewers. The `*` catch-all owner only reaches application source, because every config, prompt, release, infra and k8s path has a more specific owner.\n- **C02.3 holds.** The emergency deploy fails unless it has an owner, a reason, a parseable end date and a `hotfix/*` branch. It then opens a follow-up review issue assigned to the owner.\n\nTwo weaknesses in the emergency path didn't make me fail it, and I put them in the C02.3 note:\n- The follow-up issue step only runs if the rollout succeeds, so a deploy that is applied but then fails its rollout opens no issue.\n- The end date isn't checked to be in the future.\n\nIf you want a stricter reading, the first of those could justify failing C02.3."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1304,"cache_read_input_tokens":30029,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1304},"output_tokens":483,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a72ecefd-b4b9-4937-80c1-5